import "~/index.css";
---

<Html
  title="There Is No Laptop Anymore — Thoughts"
  og={{
    type: "article",
    title: "There Is No Laptop Anymore",
    description:
      "How I build and ship software entirely from a Claude Code cloud environment, on Nix and Cloudflare, for $30 a month.",
    publishedTime: new Date(2026, 8, 13),
    tags: ["technology", "infrastructure", "cloudflare", "nix"],
  }}
>
  <Body>
    <Nav client:load current="Thoughts" />
    <Main>
      <article>
        <Header>There Is No Laptop Anymore</Header>
        <Paragraph>
          There is no laptop anymore. Not one I develop on, at least. I haven't
          opened a terminal on it in months, and I still ship code often. The
          machine doing the programming is a Claude Code environment running in
          the cloud, one I've configured once: it installs Nix on startup,
          carries my own git identity so commits land as mine and not some
          anonymous bot, and drops into my repo with everything already wired
          up.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          The bootstrap itself is almost nothing. A short script runs once when
          the cloud environment starts: it installs Nix (no init system needed,
          since this is a container, not a full OS), adds direnv on top of it,
          and flips one Claude Code setting so it stops appending a
          "Co-Authored-By: Claude" trailer to my commits. My name and email are
          already the author and committer; I don't need the tool announcing
          itself in every commit message too. That's the entire setup.
          Everything else, the toolchains, the secrets, the deploy scripts,
          lives in the repos themselves, not in this script.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          That last part is the one I care most about. Nix is what makes the
          environment disposable. Each of my repos ships its own `flake.nix`
          declaring the exact toolchain it needs: Node, pnpm, whatever CLI
          scripts I've wrapped as first-class commands. Paired with direnv, the
          moment the agent `cd`s into the project, that toolchain is just there.
          No global installs, no "works on my machine," no drift between what I
          had last week and what I have today. The environment is cattle, not a
          pet. If the cloud session disappears, the next one reconstructs itself
          from the repo in seconds.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          Secrets travel the same way. Every project keeps a sops-encrypted{" "}
          <code>secrets.yaml</code>, dual-encrypted for my own machine and for
          Claude's cloud, checked into the repo like any other file. The cloud
          environment itself carries the matching decryption key as one of its
          own environment variables, set once when I configured it. So a fresh{
            " "
          }
          <code>git clone</code> in that environment already has everything it needs:
          no key file to fetch from somewhere else, no separate secrets manager to
          authenticate against first. Credentials load automatically through direnv
          the moment I'm in the directory. I never paste an API token into a chat,
          never keep one in some untracked <code>.env</code> file I'll inevitably
          lose. The repo is self-contained, secrets included.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          The stack on top of that is deliberately boring. TypeScript, Vite or
          Astro, React for whatever UI the project needs. Static output. The
          "backend," when a project needs one, is a Cloudflare Worker: same
          language, same repo, same deploy step. There's no server to patch, no
          OS to update, no process to keep alive. I write a function, it runs on
          the edge when someone requests it, and it costs nothing when nobody
          does.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          Which gets to the part people usually ask about first: what this
          costs. Thirty dollars a month for the Claude subscription, plus
          whatever a domain costs to register through Cloudflare. That's the
          whole bill. The cloud environment itself, the container all of this
          runs in, has real resources behind it and I'm not billed a cent for
          the machine. I'm only metered on tokens, and that's the part that
          still amazes me: actual engineering work, and the cost that scales is
          model usage, not compute. No VMs to rent, no hosting plans, no
          database I have to size or back up, no invoice that scales with
          traffic I'm not getting yet. I used to think of infrastructure as a
          fixed cost you accept before you've proven an idea is worth anything.
          Now there isn't one to accept.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          That changes what a side project actually is. It used to be an idea
          plus a weekend spent provisioning a server, wiring up CI, deciding
          where the database lives, remembering to renew a certificate six
          months later. Now it's just the idea. I describe what I want, the
          agent scaffolds it inside a Nix shell that's already correct, and it
          deploys to a Worker that was never not going to scale. The distance
          between "I thought of this" and "this is live at a real URL" has
          gotten small enough that the infrastructure stopped being a decision I
          make and became a default I inherit.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph>
          I don't think this setup is unusual for what it uses. Nix, Cloudflare
          Workers, sops, direnv are not new. What's changed is that I no longer
          need to be the one operating any of it. The agent reads the same
          `flake.nix` and `CLAUDE.md` I would, and it just runs the commands. My
          laptop's job shrank down to a terminal I open to check in on
          something, not a machine I develop on. The actual computer doing the
          work is one I don't own, don't patch, and don't pay for beyond the
          subscription.
        </Paragraph>

        <Paragraph className="mt-8 text-sm text-gray-600 italic">
          Note: The thoughts, substance, and structure of this article are mine.
          AI worked on the prose, word choice, and connectors to improve clarity
          and articulation.
        </Paragraph>
      </article>
    </Main>
    <Footer />
  </Body>
</Html>
